  "info": {
    "name": "African Alpinoid ",
    "description": "<td valign=\"top\" width=\"450\">\n<h3> Description:</h3>\r\nThe most brachycephalic Maghrebi type, shows close morphological similarities to Alpines and in a lower degree to Armenids and Berberids. Probably not directly related to other Alpines, but the result of a similar adaption. Common in the Atlas Mountains of Algeria and Tunisia. A subvariety exists on the island of Djerba. Extends in lower frequencies across Libya, Egypt, and Morocco.\r\n<h3> Physical Traits:</h3>\r\nLight brown skin, straight or wavy brown to black hair, usually dark, sometimes mixed eyes. Medium height, mesoskelic, endomorph. Rather brachycephalic, orthocranic with a leptorrhine, straight or convex nose. Features more angular than in other Alpinids, deep set eyes. <br/>\n<h3> Literature:</h3>\r\nThe name \"African Alpines\" was coined by <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#B55\" target=\"_blank\">Briggs (1955)</a>. <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#V71\" target=\"_blank\">Vallois (1971) </a> and <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#E34\" target=\"_blank\">Eickstedt (1934) </a>,who reported them especially in Berber populations, called them Alpinid. <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#G57\" target=\"_blank\">Gerhardt (1957) </a> used the name \"Brachymorph Cromagnids\"and <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#B67\" target=\"_blank\">Biasutti (1967) </a> \"Brachymorphs\". <a href=\"http://humanphenotypes.net/links.html#B13\" target=\"_blank\">Bertholon and Chantre's (1913) </a>  Gerba type can be included. </td>"
  },
